  "response": "Ran `crimes context src/billing.ts` — 8 file-local findings (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), plus three cross-file findings from the repo scan that name `src/billing.ts` as the other half.\n\nTwo clues from `context` shape the whole plan:\n\n- **blast radius: 0 direct importers.** Nothing imports `billing.ts`. Restructuring it is unusually cheap — no caller migration.\n- **test gap: bottom-quartile**, and the two tests that exist are hollow: `crime_00028` (\"renders the enterprise plan\", 0 expect calls) and `crime_00034` (\"creates an invoice\", 1 weak matcher — it asserts `toBeTruthy()` on a local stub, not on `generateInvoice`). So there is currently **no regression net** for anything below.\n\n## 1. Break up `generateInvoice` — and delete the duplicated tax table on the way out\n\n`crime_00036` (God Function, severity 0.90 / confidence 0.95): 204 lines, 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold, lines 50–253.\n\nIt's the top pick not because it's long but because it's the trunk four other findings hang off. Reading the body, one function owns: line pricing + plan discounts, a hidden `addon_` rule that silently bypasses the plan discount, loyalty tiers, promo-code resolution, fraud scoring, country tax, currency selection, object assembly, PII logging, metric emission, email-body rendering, and a retry loop. There are no seams, so an agent asked to \"change the BLACKFRIDAY promo\" has to load and re-validate tax and fraud logic it never intended to touch.\n\nExtracting pure helpers (`priceLines`, `loyaltyDiscount`, `resolvePromo`, `scoreRisk`, `taxFor`, `currencyFor`, `renderInvoiceEmail`) also directly retires:\n\n- `crime_00039` (God File, 311 lines vs 300 threshold) — the helpers move out.\n- Most of `crime_00014` (Temporal Recklessness, 7 clock reads at lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273). Once `daysSince` is computed by a helper taking `now: number`, the clock reads collapse to one at the orchestrator boundary. The finding notes none of them decide a branch — but `daysSince` at line 92 feeds both the loyalty tiers *and* the `daysSince < 1` fraud rule, which is a branch on the clock in everything but name.\n- The **duplicated country-tax table**: lines 143–151 inside `generateInvoice` and `tax()` at line 268 encode the same seven rates. No detector fires on this pair directly, but it's the same failure `crime_00004` (\"enterprise\" scattered across 4 files) is warning about — policy that exists in more than one place drifts. A rate change today needs two edits and nothing makes that obvious.\n\n## 2. Reconcile the split-brain `Invoice` / `User` contracts\n\n`crime_00011` (Contract Split-Brain, severity **0.96** — highest in the repo) and `crime_00010` (severity 0.90).\n\n`src/api/state.ts:9` declares `Invoice` with 2 fields; `src/billing.ts:19` declares `Invoice` with 11. The six absent fields are exactly the ones that matter: `userId` (identifier), `currency`, `subtotalCents`, `discountCents`, `taxCents` (money), `createdAt` (timestamp). `User` disagrees on `createdAt` — which is the field `generateInvoice` uses to compute every loyalty discount.\n\nNothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other, which is what makes this an agent trap rather than a style nit: an agent that opens `api/state.ts` first will believe an invoice is `{id, totalCents}` and write code that drops currency and tax silently — no type error, wrong money. Fix by having one declaration reference the other (`Pick<Invoice, \"id\" | \"totalCents\">` for the API projection) so the next field change reaches both.\n\n*Caveat:* `api/state.ts` self-describes as fixture material for the naming detectors, so in this specific repo the drift may be deliberate. The detector can't see that intent, and in a real repo this ranking stands — if it is intentional here, it belongs in `.crimes/suppressions.json` with a reason, the way the commented-out-code finding already is.\n\n## 3. Stop the refund path lying about what it does\n\nThree findings describe one hazard, and it's the one that moves money:\n\n- `crime_00015` (False Identity, 0.80): `calculateRefundPreview` at lines 40–44 is named like a pure `calculate*Preview` but its entire body is `saveRefundAudit()` + `sendRefundEmail()` before returning `invoice.totalCents`. Any caller — human or agent — will treat that name as safe to call speculatively, in a render path, or in a loop. Each call writes an audit record and emails a customer about a refund that may not be happening.\n- `crime_00016` (Logic in the Alibi, 0.74): the comment directly above it says *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.\"* The detector's evidence is the point — `owner`, `plan`, `billing`, `refund` appear nowhere in the nearby code. That's an authorization rule with zero enforcement, sitting one line above a function that already sends the email.\n- `crime_00032` (Catch and Release, 0.78): the retry loop at lines 245–247 catches with **no binding at all**, so the error object isn't even reachable. Worse than losing the error: line 249 then writes `metadata.emailAttempts = \"3\"` onto the returned invoice, so a total send failure is recorded as three successful-looking attempts and the caller returns normally.\n\nFix: rename to `issueRefund` (or split the pure total out from the effects), encode the owner/enterprise rule as a guard that throws or returns a typed denial, and give the catch a binding that either rethrows or records the failure on the invoice as a failure.\n\n---\n\n**Sequencing.** Do the assertions before #1. `crime_00028` and `crime_00034` mean `billing.test.ts` currently protects nothing, and the 204-line extraction is where a silent arithmetic regression is most likely. Pin `generateInvoice`'s output for a handful of `(plan, country, promo, paymentMethod)` combinations first — that's ~30 minutes and it converts #1 from risky to mechanical.\n\n**Not in the top 3, deliberately.** The commented-out legacy-refund block (lines 33–37) is already suppressed with a stated expiry — but `crime_00021` still fires, because the suppression fingerprint in `.crimes/suppressions.json` is `commented_out_code::src/billing.ts::` while the finding's is `commented_out_code::src/billing.ts::::42a4bbb39f1f`. The discriminator was added since that file was written at 0.5.0, so the suppression no longer matches. Worth a look independent of this refactor. `crime_00042` (4 TODO markers) is real but each one is a label on a problem already covered above.",
